class Solution {
    public double findMaxAverage(int[] nums, int k) {
        // Compute the sum of the first k elements
        int windowsum=0;
        for(int i=0; i<k; i++){
            windowsum+=nums[i];
        }

        int maxsum = windowsum; // Initialize maxSum with the first window

        // Slide the window across the array
        for(int i=k; i<nums.length; i++){
            windowsum+=nums[i]-nums[i-k]; // add new element, remove old
            maxsum=Math.max(maxsum, windowsum);
        }

        return (double) maxsum/k;

    }
}


Complexity
Time: O(n) (single pass with sliding window).
Space: O(1) (just variables).


If we don’t use the sliding window technique, most of these problems become much less efficient because we’d have to recompute values for 
every possible subarray or substring from scratch.

public double findMaxAverage(int[] nums, int k) {
    double maxAvg = Double.NEGATIVE_INFINITY;
    for (int i = 0; i <= nums.length - k; i++) {
        int sum = 0;
        for (int j = i; j < i + k; j++) {
            sum += nums[j];
        }
        maxAvg = Math.max(maxAvg, (double) sum / k);
    }
    return maxAvg;
}

Without sliding window: brute force, repetitive calculations, O(n²) or worse.
